2. The biggest architectural problem

❌ Host branches are being treated as both configuration sources and runtime state channels

This is the part I would change.

You currently have:

main
 ├── host-linux-laptop
 └── host-win-desktop

and then:

machine
   ↓
detect state
   ↓
commit
   ↓
push host branch

The danger is that your host branches become mutable state databases.

Eventually you'll get:

main
  │
  ├── desired configuration
  │
  └── shared improvements
        ↓
host-linux
  │
  ├── desired configuration
  ├── machine-specific configuration
  ├── generated state
  └── accidental changes

That becomes difficult to reason about.

Better model

Separate:

Desired state

from

Machine identity/state

For example:

dotfiles/
├── common/
│   ├── zsh/
│   ├── nvim/
│   └── git/
│
├── hosts/
│   ├── linux/
│   │   └── laptop/
│   └── windows/
│       └── desktop/
│
├── scripts/
├── modules/
├── tests/
└── .github/

Then the machine determines:

OS
HOST_ID
PROFILE

and the installer composes:

common + host-specific

rather than using Git branches as the fundamental configuration hierarchy.

My strong recommendation: use branches for workflow/isolation, but use directories/profiles for configuration composition.


