3. main protection is incomplete

Your document says:

> Directly pushing or force-pushing to main ... is completely blocked by automated Git hooks. 



This is conceptually wrong.

A Git hook is client-side, not a security boundary.

Anyone can:

rm .git/hooks/pre-push

or:

git push --no-verify

or push from another machine.

Therefore:

local hook
     ≠
repository protection

Correct architecture

Use GitHub branch protection/rulesets as the authoritative enforcement:

Developer
   │
   ▼
host branch
   │
   ▼
PR
   │
   ├── CI
   ├── tests
   ├── review
   └── policy
        │
        ▼
      main

Keep the local hook as a developer safety mechanism, but not as the security mechanism.


