9. Automatic commits need a policy

The plan says:

git add .
git commit -m "Automated state checkpoint..."
git push



This is dangerous because:

git add .

means everything.

Your dotfiles repository could accidentally capture:

secrets
tokens
machine-generated files
credentials
cache files
private configs
temporary files
large binaries

You need explicit boundaries.

I'd establish:

TRACKED
├── configuration
├── intentional machine state
└── generated metadata

NEVER TRACK
├── credentials
├── secrets
├── caches
├── logs
├── runtime state
└── arbitrary $HOME files

And ideally use an allow-list rather than relying purely on .gitignore.


